module gunbc.durable_cas_file_store

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String, Int, Bool }
import std.content_hash { ContentHash, content_hash_of_value, compare_content_hash, ContentHashEqual, ContentHashDifferent, ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable }
import std.durable_compare_and_set { CasAttempt, CasOutcome, CasCommitted, CasPreconditionFailed, CasStoreRefused, CasExpectation, ExpectSlotAbsent, ExpectSlotGeneration, CasGeneration, CasSlotVersion, CasReadableAbsent, CasReadablePresent, CasSlotObservation, CasObservedReadable, CasObservedUnreadable, CasUnreadableMalformed, CasUnreadableReadRefused, cas_generation_first, cas_generation_next, cas_generation_count }
import extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io { Filesystem }

// The first production realization of std.durable_compare_and_set: one keyed
// slot backed by generation-suffixed files, exclusion supplied by O_EXCL.
//
// WHY THIS SHAPE. The interface module states the realization it wants --
// "folding read and conditional-write into ONE store-owned operation so no
// caller ever supplies an observation separately", with the store reading
// attempt.key itself. That is what file_compare_and_set is. The caller hands
// over an attempt and never an observation, so the declared key-relation
// boundary obligation is CLOSED HERE BY CONSTRUCTION rather than observed and
// refused: there is no second argument in which a caller could supply an
// observation read from another slot.
//
// WHY GENERATION-SUFFIXED FILES, which is the whole mechanism. A slot at
// generation N is the file <root>/<key>.<N>, written once and never rewritten.
// Then BOTH expectations collapse into the same primitive:
//
//   ExpectSlotAbsent          -> create-new <key>.1
//   ExpectSlotGeneration{N}   -> create-new <key>.<N+1>
//
// and create-new is O_EXCL, which fails if the path exists. So the exclusion
// is performed by the operating system on the write itself, not by a lock the
// caller holds. That is the gap the interface module names: the exclusion in
// fleet_converge_plan_cli is flock, which is single-host by construction and
// cannot serialize two writers on different hosts. O_EXCL on a shared
// filesystem can -- BUT ONLY AGAINST THE SAME STORE INSTANCE, and the premise
// matters enough to state rather than imply. O_EXCL serializes two writers when
// they address one store: a shared filesystem whose exclusive-create semantics
// are trusted, or a single control plane performing the operation. Two LOCAL
// roots on two hosts are two independent stores and exclude nothing from each
// other. No update-in-place operation is needed at all, and one is deliberately
// not offered.
//
// THE ORDERING, CORRECTED. An earlier revision of this module read "the write
// DECIDES and the read only REPORTS", and computed the target generation from
// the expectation alone before writing. That is sound for ExpectSlotAbsent and
// WRONG for ExpectSlotGeneration, because O_EXCL excludes competing writers for
// the TARGET PATH and establishes nothing about which generation is currently
// the head.
//
// The counterexample that retired it: an EMPTY store, an attempt expecting
// generation 7. The target is 8, no key.8 exists, the exclusive create succeeds,
// and the old code reported CasCommitted -- committing on a precondition that
// was never true. Likewise a slot at generation 3 expecting 7: key.8 is free, so
// the write wins and skips 4 through 7.
//
// THE CORRECT INVARIANT IS: THE READ ESTABLISHES ELIGIBILITY, THE EXCLUSIVE
// WRITE DECIDES THE WINNER. Reading first does NOT reopen a time-of-check to
// time-of-use race, which is the objection that produced the wrong ordering in
// the first place: if two writers both observe head N, both derive target N+1,
// and both attempt an exclusive create, exactly one succeeds. The race is
// resolved by the write exactly as before -- what the read adds is that a writer
// whose expectation never matched the store is refused instead of winning.
//
// So the sequence is: observe the slot, compare the observation against the
// expectation, derive the target from the OBSERVATION, create it exclusively,
// and only then report a commit.

// WHY THE FAILED WRITE IS DISAMBIGUATED BY A SECOND OBSERVATION rather than
// by reading write.error. "The path already exists" and "the store refused the
// write" are different outcomes with opposite remedies -- one is a lost race
// to be retried against the new generation, the other is an operational fault
// -- and the transport reports both as success:false with a String. Choosing
// between them by matching on that String would be the anemic leaf DESIGN 2
// names: a caller parsing prose to pick a remedy. Observing the target instead
// is a structural test. It is sound precisely BECAUSE slot files are
// append-only: a generation file is never rewritten and never deleted, so if
// the read sees it, it is the real winner and not a value that might vanish.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. It does not create <root>; a missing root
// surfaces as a store refusal rather than being silently created, because
// creating the directory that defines the control plane's identity is a
// convergence decision and not a side effect of one attempt. It offers no
// delete and no rewrite, so the append-only invariant the disambiguation above
// depends on is preserved by the absence of an operation rather than by a rule
// anyone has to follow.

// The verified attempt, and the reason it exists is a gap this realization
// found in the interface by being the first thing to implement it.
//
// CasOutcome has exactly three arms -- committed, precondition failed, store
// refused -- and NONE of them can say "the attempt's proposed_content is not
// the digest of its proposed value". Reporting that as CasStoreRefused would
// blame the store for a caller's inconsistency, which is the state-space
// conflation DESIGN names: two states with opposite owners and opposite
// repairs collapsed onto one symbol.
//
// The interface module says the pairing "cannot be closed by construction in a
// pure generic fold" because content_hash_of_value is not generic over T, and
// that is correct THERE. It does not bind HERE: this realization is concrete
// at NonEmptyStr, which is exactly the type content_hash_of_value accepts, so
// the digest IS derivable from the payload at this layer. The interface also
// names where the duty belongs -- "the obligation belongs to the immutable
// object store that realizes this interface: it holds the bytes, so it can
// verify bytes against digest" -- and this is that store.
//
// So rather than widen CasOutcome, the input is narrowed. Verification happens
// at the mint, and file_compare_and_set accepts only the minted form; the
// unverified pair is not refused downstream, it is unwritable there. This is
// the same move the interface made when it narrowed CasSlotObservation to
// CasReadableSlot -- deleting arms instead of checking them.
//
// sole_constructor is the wall: admit_cas_attempt is the only mint, so no
// caller in any other module can produce a VerifiedCasAttempt by writing the
// record literal. RUNG: structurally guaranteed on the source-to-.dag
// acceptance path, which is the path this store is consumed on. It is NOT
// claimed for the emitted-Rust path, where DESIGN records by execution that a
// sole_constructor type's emitted mirror is forgeable; that is a known gap in
// the wall itself and not a property of this carrier.
type VerifiedCasAttempt sole_constructor {
  attempt: CasAttempt<NonEmptyStr>
}

// Why a digest disagreement and a cross-family pair are separate arms: they
// have different repairs. A mismatch means the caller paired one value with
// another value's digest and the fix is in the caller. Incomparable means the
// two hashes are from different families and no comparison was possible at
// all, so nothing was checked -- an absence of evidence, and reporting it as a
// mismatch would be a verdict the store never reached. compare_content_hash
// exists precisely to refuse that collapse, and consuming it while flattening
// its third arm would discard the distinction it was built to preserve.
// THE KEY MUST NAME A SLOT, NOT A PATH, and this arm is a defect found by
// building the realization rather than by reading the model.
//
// cas_file_slot_path interpolates the key straight into a filesystem path. A
// key containing a slash therefore stops being an identifier and becomes
// navigation: "../../etc/passwd" writes OUTSIDE the store root entirely, and
// any key with a slash also needs directories this store deliberately does not
// create. Neither is a store fault to be reported -- both are keys that should
// never have reached the store.
//
// REFUSING THE SLASH IS SUFFICIENT, and the reason is worth stating because it
// is not obvious. A bare ".." is harmless here: the generation suffix is ALWAYS
// appended, so ".." becomes the file "...1" and can never be the final path
// component. Traversal needs a separator to climb, so removing the separator
// removes the class -- this is one predicate, not a blocklist of dangerous
// spellings, and a blocklist would be the heuristic DESIGN 4 says a closed
// system never needs.
type CasAttemptAdmission
  = CasAttemptAdmitted { verified: VerifiedCasAttempt }
  | CasAttemptKeyNotSlotAddressable { key: NonEmptyStr }
  | CasAttemptDigestMismatch { claimed: ContentHash, actual: ContentHash }
  | CasAttemptDigestIncomparable { claimed: ContentHash, actual: ContentHash }

fn cas_key_is_slot_addressable(key: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  !(key as String).contains("/")
}

fn admit_cas_attempt(attempt: CasAttempt<NonEmptyStr>) -> CasAttemptAdmission {
  if !cas_key_is_slot_addressable(key: attempt.key) {
    CasAttemptKeyNotSlotAddressable { key: attempt.key }
  } else {
    cas_admit_verified_digest(attempt: attempt)
  }
}

fn cas_admit_verified_digest(attempt: CasAttempt<NonEmptyStr>) -> CasAttemptAdmission {
  let actual = content_hash_of_value(value: attempt.proposed)
  match compare_content_hash(left: attempt.proposed_content, right: actual) {
    ContentHashEqual => CasAttemptAdmitted { verified: VerifiedCasAttempt { attempt: attempt } }
    ContentHashDifferent => CasAttemptDigestMismatch { claimed: attempt.proposed_content, actual: actual }
    ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable => CasAttemptDigestIncomparable { claimed: attempt.proposed_content, actual: actual }
  }
}

// The path a slot's generation occupies. The generation is IN the path, which
// is what lets an exclusive create serve as the conditional write: two
// generations of one slot are two different files, so the OS decides the race.
fn cas_file_slot_path(root: NonEmptyStr, key: NonEmptyStr, generation: CasGeneration) -> String {
  concat(concat(concat(concat(root as String, "/"), key as String), "."), cas_generation_count(g: generation) as String)
}

// HOW THE SLOT IS OBSERVED, AND THE BOUND IS PART OF THE CONTRACT.
//
// Generations are contiguous and append-only, so the head is found by probing
// upward from 1 until a generation is absent. That needs only Read, no
// directory listing and no parsing of names back into numbers -- a filesystem
// layout that had to be re-parsed to be understood would be a second identity
// authority beside the key.
//
// THE PROBE IS BOUNDED AND THE BOUND REFUSES RATHER THAN TRUNCATING. Running off
// the end returns a typed refusal, not "the head is wherever I stopped looking":
// a walk that silently reported its own patience as the answer would be the
// bound-shaped closure DESIGN names, where a real number satisfies a question it
// never measured.
//
// DECLARED BOUNDARY, INHERITED FROM THE TRANSPORT RATHER THAN INTRODUCED HERE:
// Filesystem.Read reports success and an error string, and cannot distinguish
// "this generation does not exist" from "this generation could not be read".
// The probe therefore treats an unreadable generation as the end of the chain.
// Choosing between them by matching on the error string would be the anemic leaf
// DESIGN forbids, so the conflation is declared instead of papered over. RUNG:
// mitigatable. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a filesystem read operation whose absence arm
// is a typed outcome rather than a false success flag.
type CasSlotProbe
  = ProbedAbsent
  | ProbedHead { generation: CasGeneration, value: NonEmptyStr }
  | ProbedBeyondBound { bound: Int }

data cas_max_generation_probe: Int = 4096

fn cas_probe_from(root: NonEmptyStr, key: NonEmptyStr, generation: CasGeneration, previous: NonEmptyStr) -> CasSlotProbe {
  if cas_generation_count(g: generation) > cas_max_generation_probe {
    ProbedBeyondBound { bound: cas_max_generation_probe }
  } else {
    let read = Filesystem.Read(path: cas_file_slot_path(root: root, key: key, generation: generation))
    if read.success {
      cas_probe_from(root: root, key: key, generation: cas_generation_next(g: generation), previous: read.content as NonEmptyStr)
    } else {
      if cas_generation_count(g: generation) == 1 {
        ProbedAbsent
      } else {
        ProbedHead { generation: cas_generation_count(g: generation) - 1, value: previous }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn cas_observe_slot(root: NonEmptyStr, key: NonEmptyStr) -> CasSlotProbe {
  cas_probe_from(root: root, key: key, generation: cas_generation_first(), previous: "unread" as NonEmptyStr)
}

fn cas_probe_as_readable(probe: CasSlotProbe) -> CasReadableSlot<NonEmptyStr> {
  match probe {
    ProbedAbsent => CasReadableAbsent
    ProbedHead { generation: h, value: v } =>
      CasReadablePresent {
        version: CasSlotVersion { generation: h, content: content_hash_of_value(value: v), value: v }
      }
    ProbedBeyondBound { bound: _ } => CasReadableAbsent
  }
}

// THE STORE'S OBSERVATION, FOR CALLERS THAT MUST DECIDE BEFORE THEY WRITE.
//
// file_compare_and_set needs no such surface: it observes and decides in one
// step, and its callers hand it an attempt rather than an observation. A
// caller that has to answer a question about the slot BEFORE proposing
// anything -- is this cell held, and by whom -- has no way to ask, and hand
// building an observation to feed such a decision would make the caller the
// author of the fact it is supposed to be reading.
//
// THE BOUND ARM IS WHY THIS IS NOT cas_probe_as_readable. That projection
// answers CasReadableAbsent for a slot that exceeded the probe bound, which is
// correct for its own use -- reporting what beat a lost race -- and wrong here:
// a decision caller would read "the slot holds nothing" from a store that could
// not tell it anything, and choose the remedy for an empty cell when the actual
// remedy is to repair the slot. That is the state-space conflation this corpus
// keeps finding, so the bound arm becomes unreadable-with-a-cause and only the
// two genuinely readable arms delegate.
fn observe_cas_slot_state(root: NonEmptyStr, key: NonEmptyStr) -> CasSlotObservation<NonEmptyStr> {
  let probe = cas_observe_slot(root: root, key: key)
  match probe {
    ProbedBeyondBound { bound: b } =>
      CasObservedUnreadable {
        cause: CasUnreadableMalformed {
          detail: concat("slot exceeds the generation probe bound of ", b as String) as NonEmptyStr
        }
      }
    ProbedAbsent => CasObservedReadable { readable: cas_probe_as_readable(probe: probe) }
    ProbedHead { generation: _, value: _ } =>
      CasObservedReadable { readable: cas_probe_as_readable(probe: probe) }
  }
}

// The exclusive create. Reached only once the observation has already satisfied
// the expectation, so this decides WHO WINS among eligible writers and nothing
// else. Losing here is a lost race, not a bad precondition -- another writer
// took the same target between our observation and our write -- so the slot is
// re-observed to report what actually beat us rather than guessing.
fn cas_commit_at(root: NonEmptyStr, attempt: CasAttempt<NonEmptyStr>, target: CasGeneration) -> CasOutcome<NonEmptyStr> {
  let write = Filesystem.WriteOwnerOnly(
    path: cas_file_slot_path(root: root, key: attempt.key, generation: target),
    content: attempt.proposed as String,
  )
  if write.success {
    CasCommitted {
      committed: CasSlotVersion { generation: target, content: attempt.proposed_content, value: attempt.proposed }
    }
  } else {
    CasPreconditionFailed {
      expected: attempt.expected,
      observed: cas_probe_as_readable(probe: cas_observe_slot(root: root, key: attempt.key)),
    }
  }
}

// The store-owned compare-and-set. It reads attempt.key itself; no caller
// supplies an observation, which is what closes the key-relation obligation.
//
// THE TARGET GENERATION IS DERIVED FROM THE OBSERVATION, NEVER FROM THE
// EXPECTATION. That is the whole correction: an expectation is a claim about the
// store, and deriving the write target from the claim rather than from the store
// is what let an attempt expecting generation 7 commit against an empty slot.
fn file_compare_and_set(root: NonEmptyStr, verified: VerifiedCasAttempt) -> CasOutcome<NonEmptyStr> {
  let attempt = verified.attempt
  let probe = cas_observe_slot(root: root, key: attempt.key)
  match probe {
    ProbedBeyondBound { bound: b } =>
      CasStoreRefused {
        cause: CasUnreadableMalformed {
          detail: concat("slot exceeds the generation probe bound of ", b as String) as NonEmptyStr
        }
      }
    ProbedAbsent =>
      match attempt.expected {
        ExpectSlotAbsent => cas_commit_at(root: root, attempt: attempt, target: cas_generation_first())
        ExpectSlotGeneration { generation: _ } =>
          CasPreconditionFailed { expected: attempt.expected, observed: CasReadableAbsent }
      }
    ProbedHead { generation: h, value: v } =>
      match attempt.expected {
        ExpectSlotAbsent =>
          CasPreconditionFailed { expected: attempt.expected, observed: cas_probe_as_readable(probe: probe) }
        ExpectSlotGeneration { generation: g } =>
          if cas_generation_count(g: g) == cas_generation_count(g: h) {
            cas_commit_at(root: root, attempt: attempt, target: cas_generation_next(g: h))
          } else {
            CasPreconditionFailed { expected: attempt.expected, observed: cas_probe_as_readable(probe: probe) }
          }
      }
  }
}
